questions.question:
Consider these two similar-looking queries:

// Query 1: Single MATCH
MATCH (p:Person {name: "Al Pacino"})-[:ACTED_IN]->(m:Movie)<-[:ACTED_IN]-(costar)
RETURN costar.name
ORDER BY count(m) DESC, costar.name
LIMIT 1;

// Query 2: Separate MATCH clauses
MATCH (p:Person {name: "Al Pacino"})-[:ACTED_IN]->(m:Movie)
MATCH (m)<-[:ACTED_IN]-(costar)
RETURN costar.name
ORDER BY count(m) DESC, costar.name
LIMIT 1;

Query 1 never returns Al Pacino as a costar, but Query 2 can. Why?

Complete the Cypher statement below to pass the p identifier to the subquery.

MATCH (p:Person)-[:ACTED_IN]->()-[:IN_GENRE]->(:Genre {name: 'Comedy'})
CALL -- select -- {
	SET p:FunnyActor
} IN TRANSACTIONS OF 1000 ROWS
